#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7f3646c9-89b6-58de-a363-cb87e034d9f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5333" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/d05aa50677877b505dd8f37cac612630.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4)$, we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(-8) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\\ny^{2} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d66f0111-fe31-5381-ab6d-cd67ee403d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-5334" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(4) \\\\\n16 &= 16p \\\\\np &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(1)x \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5546750b-01ad-5aaa-93de-b223297ffc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5335" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/ba4f53d4bcb8d7c3ce8e264ba354d250.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4,2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6)$, so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(0 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(-1)(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -4(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "9a27e39b-4a17-578f-830b-3e143c65fd55" :question/math-academy-id "e-5336" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (2,-2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2)$, so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(2 + 2)^{2} &= 4p(-6 - 2) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= -2(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a282ecd-9120-5164-9142-e8fed956f6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=-\\frac{3}{8}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266689} {:answer/id #uuid "01480733-3dea-5b7a-a998-8e6987364ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266690} {:answer/id #uuid "90da7ecc-92e5-566f-b1ae-aa71cd79d6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266691} {:answer/id #uuid "6faf3cff-1208-501c-bd13-4ddc53d72dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266692} {:answer/id #uuid "3a407151-85b6-55e5-94c1-77ac3be02ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=12(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a407151-85b6-55e5-94c1-77ac3be02ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=12(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693} :answer-field/id #uuid "094bad5d-3056-51a0-b6a9-d51a378c2415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7d690cd-8b12-547d-a0c5-09b9dbca554a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115640" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and passes through the point $(6,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,3),$ we substitute $h=3$ and $k=3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (-3-3)^{2} & =4p(6-3) \\\\ 36 & =12p \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(3)(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=3$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb3077d-eeda-5dc3-b79c-f796b335c13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266717} {:answer/id #uuid "75105eb6-7716-5a06-9451-922804cb471e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266718} {:answer/id #uuid "c641f368-a0eb-5fa6-a43a-b2f490b47a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266719} {:answer/id #uuid "20710e9e-58c5-5498-a5ba-a5e4cafe2e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266720} {:answer/id #uuid "7787c439-3e4b-538a-8ac5-e345b8994bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb3077d-eeda-5dc3-b79c-f796b335c13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266717} :answer-field/id #uuid "55dfbc75-e828-5b1d-95c8-840d0634b2eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2663faff-8b8b-5a3f-b808-80a34f1d85cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11751" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 64 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-2)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295f8b08-db31-5d74-8c49-7680c33eb4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266752} {:answer/id #uuid "316676c5-b207-5ca0-8eb7-7e127d6a4655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266753} {:answer/id #uuid "7839009f-cb3b-561b-87c2-05a125ad1ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266754} {:answer/id #uuid "1da2cb8f-f928-529d-a370-2dbf505caaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266755} {:answer/id #uuid "fc491c15-266a-58c7-a2f3-b2b562f21758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "316676c5-b207-5ca0-8eb7-7e127d6a4655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266753} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb26fbb-2bef-540d-bb2c-0db2167556de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3122981-1a91-5414-84c6-5eaaa9c218b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11794" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,4)$ and passes through the point $(-1,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,4),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (8-4)^{2} & =4p(-1-1) \\\\ 16 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-2)(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-8(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
